  "explanation": {
    "markdown": "## Verbs + **\\-ing**\n\nSome verbs in English are followed by another verb in the **\\-ing** form. The most common of these verbs are [verbs of liking](https://test-english.com/vocabulary/a2/hobbies-and-free-time-a2-english-vocabulary/) and disliking: **love, like, enjoy, don’t mind, don’t like, hate**. If we use another verb after these verbs, it often takes the **\\-ing** form.\n\n-   *I **love going** to the gym.* \n-   *I **like reading**.* \n-   *I **enjoy travelling**.* \n-   *I **don’t mind cooking**.* \n-   *I **don’t like doing** homework.* \n-   *I **hate getting** up early.* \n\nThe second verb is in the **\\-ing** form in the past, too.\n\n-   *When I was a child, I **hated going** to school. ![](/uploads/grammar/a1/verbs-infinitive-verbs-ing/img-1.png)* \n-   *When I was a child, I **hated went** to school. ![](/uploads/grammar/a1/verbs-infinitive-verbs-ing/img-2.png)* \n\n#### **\\-ing** spelling\n\nYou can check the spelling of the **\\-ing** form of the verbs in the table below.\n\n![Educational chart displaying spelling rules for adding '-ing' to verbs, with examples for each rule.](/uploads/grammar/a1/verbs-infinitive-verbs-ing/img-3.png)\n\n## Verbs + **to**\\-infinitive\n\nMany verbs in English are followed by **to**\\-infinitive. Some of these verbs are **forget, hope, learn, need, offer, plan, remember, start, want** and **would like**.\n\n-   *I **forgot to turn off** the light.* \n-   *I **hope to see** you soon.* \n-   *I**‘m learning to drive**.* \n-   *We **need to arrive** early.* \n-   *She **offered to help** us.* \n-   *We **are planning to go** to France.* \n-   ***Remember to lock** the door.* \n-   *I **want to go** home.* \n-   *I **would like to buy** a new computer.* \n\nThe second verb takes the **to**\\-infinitive form in the past too.\n\n-   *I **wanted to go** home. ![](/uploads/grammar/a1/verbs-infinitive-verbs-ing/img-4.png)* \n-   *I **wanted to went** home. ![](/uploads/grammar/a1/verbs-infinitive-verbs-ing/img-5.png)* \n\n#### Would like\n\nThe verb [**would like**](https://test-english.com/grammar-points/a1/would-like/) (or **‘d like**) is different from **like**. We use **would like + to** \\+ infinitive, and we normally use **like** + **\\-ing** verb to talk about general preference.\n\n-   *I**‘d like to study** English.* \n-   *I **like studying** English.* \n\nThe meaning is also different.\n\n-   *I**‘d like to study** English.* (=I want to do it.)\n-   *I **like studying** English.* (=I enjoy it.)\n\nWe use the question **would you like…** when we offer or suggest something.\n\n-   ***Would you like** a cup of coffee?*\n-   ***Would you like** to dance with me?*",
